calibration and  these frames  don't capture the image but they do  capture the noise  and there's basically three types of  them uh there's something called a dark  frame and uh these are taken  uh the same length as your light frames  um and the camera needs to be the same  temperature as the light frames  but with the camera lens covered or the  telescope lens covered  and uh what this does is it me uh it  measures the amount of  noise generated by the heat of the  electronics inside your camera  and this is a significant uh thing even  regular dslrs do this automatically you  may not realize it  but we take them explicitly to subtract  them out  there's something called a bias frame it  turns out the actual process  of reading out the ccd wells in your  camera  has uncertainty associated with it and  these bias frames which are extremely  short so they don't have any thermal  noise  just contain the uh this offset this  noise associated with  the reed and then there's a third type  of frame called a flat  frame and these are taken um with the  aperture uncovered  uh and pointed at a  neutral evenly illuminated surface  like for instance the morning sky or the  evening sky  and sometimes you you put a cloth over  the telescope to further  even it out and the reason why we do  this is  to measure the light fall off  on on your sensor  large sensors especially those in like  digital slrs  there's noticeable light fall off  towards the edges and you have to be  able to multiply that  out this also uh takes care of  artifacts in your optical train like  dust on the sensor  you'll identify this and that will get  subtracted out  and here is a brief uh  description the raw frame you subtract  out the master bias and the master dark  frames and you divide out by the master  flat and you get a calibrated frame  and then you can take those and you can  stack them to further reduce  noise so let's give an example  of uh this deep sky imaging calibration  workflow so here i imaged m27 the  dumbbell nebula  with a small telescope an agent schmidt  casagrain  and a canon dslr  camera and this was done in my backyard  in downtown urbana  illinois in july  um in this example i i separately  process  the offset and the dark frames  um so i'm importing them you can see  master bias in master dark  and then i form a list of  the light frames these are the actual  exposures  so i'm just bringing in the names of  these frames right now  the reason why i don't immediately bring  the entire set into memory is because  they're extremely large they're high  resolution  and they're in raw format so there's no  compression whatsoever  so each one is probably at least 25  megabytes of data  so um for this example we're just going  to use seven  so i'm going to take seven out of my  list of file names and i'm going to  import them all  okay so now we're going to use the  functional programming features of  mathematica  to take the list of light frames and  subtract off that bias  master bias frame that will take off the  offset and the read noise  and then we're going to take off the  thermal noise by subtracting out the  master dark frame and we're reusing  the list of lights  here so we don't consume additional  memory  and then we're going to use the built-in  image alignment features in mathematica  which are extremely sophisticated  to to bring all the the frames into  perfect alignment because there may be  slight drifting because the telescope  isn't  exactly pointed at the polar axis  and finally we're going to use image  exposure combined to build the final  image  out of the uh collection of calibrated  light frames  and then uh the last thing we do is we  take a histogram  of m27 and we stretch  the interesting data part from about  point one to  point three so it covers the entire data  range and this is what it looks like so  this is the dumbbell nebula  the image is a little noisy uh because  you know we only use seven frames but if  i had  spent a longer period of time the noise  would go down  and the uh interesting  parts of the image would pop up  and finally oh let's go back here if you  have a good eye  you can see that i didn't use any flat  frames in this  example and you can see that the image  is noticeably darker in the corners here  of the image than it is right in the  center and that's that light fall off i  was talking about  because the sensor on the camera is  pretty big compared to  the illumination disc of the telescope  and some people uh fix this with  additional optics to flatten  stuff out uh we're just going to use  software that we have  okay also you can see um artifacts on  the sensor  luckily mathematica has a great function  for doing this  and we're not going to use any of its  sophisticated features here we're just  going to throw the image right into it  and say i do brightness equalize  and and it pretty well flattens out the  image  uh of course it doesn't take care of the  dust artifacts  for that you really need to have flat  frames so  that future examples will have that sort  of  thing  so that's an example of the deep  sky imaging workflow  again a very dim object that  you need to do a lot of noise reduction  to see properly  now we're going to move on to planetary  imaging this is completely the opposite  these are planets tend to be very small  and very bright  compared to nebula and clusters  so you need different imaging techniques  the first thing you need to do is you  need to get  much more magnification so your optical  train  will probably include stuff like barlow  lens or maybe eyepiece  projection and one of the examples here  will be using eyepiece projection  to magnify mars which is  uh notoriously difficult to image  because it's very small  so giving an idea of this difference in  scale  we can compare the size of the moon to  the size of the andromeda galaxy  the moon is about 30  arc minutes in diameter uh even though  it looks big in the sky  it's only half a degree in size for  instance uh  the andromeda galaxy even though it's  much much dimmer  is six times as large  and you can see it with your naked eye  in uh  you know a summer evening you can see it  go out and and just look in the sky and  you can see a little fuzzy patch there  that's not the entire galaxy that's just  the core of the galaxy you're seeing you  need a long exposure to bring out the  full galaxy  so for planetary imaging what we're  going to do is we're going to try to  suck as many  images into the telescope as possible  and  and align them and  stack them all this will  even out the noise and it will also  even out a problem that since we're  using high magnification the image is  probably going to be dancing around a  lot  so the ideal way to bring that sort of  data  into mathematica is with a video clip  the good things about planetary imaging  doesn't require careful alignment of the  telescope because the  alignment technique kind of takes care  of this  so you don't even need to set up your  your  star tracking your closed loop guiding  drift effects  as long as they don't you know are are  terrible  they will be canceled out  you don't need to take a number of  exposures to get a large object like the  andromeda galaxy  you can get it all in one shot noise  isn't much of a problem because  you're stacking many many images  and the equipment is also very modest  this is just a regular webcam that i  modified  to use as an astronomical camera  so i use this very camera here to take  some pictures of mars so  bring in hundreds of images um if you  want you can subtract out a dark frame  uh for each of the images and use our  image morphology  routines determine the centroid of the  circular image or centroid of the planet  and line all the images based on that  centroid  and then you combine the images using  statistical functions  so to show what this can do um we're  going to look at  really horrible data and i i have 