ta that  you can get you know just with a natural  language query  right in mathematica  this is another example this is a little  better data here  and it shows what you can do uh this is  um  289 images now this wasn't a video clip  this was just a 289  um small still images taken with a  different  astronomical camera this was a cooled  camera so there's less noise associated  with it  but once i calculated the centroid  and aligned all the images and did a  mild deconvolution  i got a pretty detailed picture of  the cloud structure on jupiter  um so uh what i've been talking to you  up to now  is about um uh basically creating uh  nice pictures of mathematica of  astronomical objects but you can  actually  uh use uh rather modest equipment  and techniques to actually get  interesting scientific data out of  um your amateur astronomy  endeavors and this i talked about this  last year i'm going to  briefly cover it again i won't go into  full detail but this sort of thing can  be used to get  interesting and important data for  instance a light curve of a variable  star  which can give us clues to how far away  it is and its intrinsic brightness  it can also be used to determine uh  things like uh  planetary crossings you can discover  exoplanets using this technique  um so this is a useful uh serious  um astronomical technique and it's not  just limited to  uh professional astronomers a lot of  amateurs uh  like to look for exoplanets and they do  it in a similar way  so the way we extract the light curve is  we have to use mathematica to divide up  the the image  and we use this by by creating kind of a  virtual  uh bullseye here there's where there's  three  interesting areas the center red disc  is where you put the star and then this  blue annulus  measures the the background uh  brightness and you subtract out the  background brightness by counting up  the pixel values and that normalizes the  star brightness to a constant value  and then by taking a series 